Comparatively and relatively are followed by an adjective in the positive degree, not the comparative degree.

1. Comparatively

Comparatively means when compared with something/someone else.

Use:

comparatively + positive adjective

Examples:

The weather is comparatively hot. ✅

❌ The weather is comparatively hotter.

2. Relatively

The same rule applies to relatively.

The train is relatively slow. ✅

❌ The train is relatively slower.

This problem is relatively easy. ✅

❌ This problem is relatively easier.

                                                      Degrees of comparison
                                            Positive	    Comparative        	Superlative
                                            hot          	 hotter	              hottest
                                            slow	         slower	              slowest
                                            good	         better	               best
                                            easy	         easier             	easiest

So:

comparatively hot ✅
relatively slow ✅
comparatively good ✅


⭐ Important distinction

Don't confuse this with “much/far + comparative”:

much better ✅
far better ✅


it does more harm than good/better??

The correct sentence is:

✅ It does more harm than good.

Not:

❌ It does more harm than better.

Why?

Here, harm and good are both nouns.

The expression is:

do more harm than good
